  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble members are in good agreement that by Tuesday drier air \nwill start to filter back into the region as the subtropical high \nstarts to retrograde back over our region. By the middle of the week \nthe center of the high will be straddling the AZ/NM border and by \nthe end of the week the center of the high will be over AZ. As the \nhigh moves overhead, PWATs will drop into a 0.8-1.2\" range across \nour region and steering flow will be out of the S/SW. The \ncombination of these factors will lead to poor chances for \nthunderstorm development across the region. The NBM shows that \nTuesday and Wednesday there could be enough moisture around to \nsupport isolated thunderstorm chances for the higher terrain areas. \nBut by the end of next week thunderstorm chances for our area shut \noff and will mainly be relegated to the Mogollon Rim. \n\nUnusually hot temperatures will be the main story next week. As \nmentioned above the subtropical high will slowly retrograde back \nover our region beginning on Tuesday, which will result in a gradual \nwarming trend for our area. The high is also expected to strengthen \nto around 596-598dam during this time. The combination of the center \nsettling over our region and strengthening will result in \ntemperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above normal for this \ntime of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) are forecasted to be in \nthe 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the lower deserts and in the \n100-110 (75-85) degree range across the higher terrain areas. The \nincrease in temperatures will also result in an increase in \nHeatRisk, with widespread extreme HeatRisk forecast for the majority \nof the CWA by Wednesday. There remains high confidence in the heat \nforecast for next week. So, the Extreme Heat Warning remains in \neffect through next Friday and will likely need to be expanded to \nmore areas by the middle of the week and may eventually need to be \nextended into next weekend.",
